LSI Logic 53C895A and 53C810 SCSI miniport, PCI 1000:0012 and 1000:0001 (QEMU: lsi53c895a and lsi53c810; the
Proxmox VE "LSI 53C895A" controller, the default of its older templates)

The driver (sym53c8xx.c) is new code, GPL-2.0-or-later, written against the legacy SCSI port interface
(scsiport) like the BusLogic and other miniports. The chip runs a SCRIPTS program that the driver loads
into the uncached extension and starts at the physical address of its first instruction.

Origin
  Project:  OpenBSD
  Files:    sys/dev/ic/siopreg.h, sys/dev/ic/siop_common.c, sys/dev/ic/siop.c (revision 1.90): the register
            names and bits, the chip set-up, the residual count after a phase mismatch and the structure of
            the interrupt handling.
            sys/dev/microcode/siop/siop.ss (revision 1.10) and its assembler ncr53cxxx.c: sym53c8xx.ss is a
            smaller SCRIPTS program derived from siop.ss (the phase dispatch, the message in handling and
            the data loop; no scheduler slots, no per-target or tag switch: one command at a time, the
            program needs no relocation). sym53c8xx_script.h is its assembled form (the assembler of the
            OpenBSD directory, invocation in the header of the .ss file; to change the program, change the
            .ss file and assemble it again).
  URL:      https://github.com/openbsd/src/tree/master/sys/dev/ic and /sys/dev/microcode/siop
  License:  BSD 2-clause, Copyright (c) 2000 Manuel Bouyer; the notice is kept in sym53c8xx.c and in
            sym53c8xx.ss
  No code of the OpenBSD driver was copied.

Not used: the Linux drivers sym53c8xx and ncr53c8xx (GPL-2.0-only, not compatible with this tree), and any
Symbios/LSI binary driver.

What it does not do: tagged queueing and more than one command at a time (the port is told about the next
request when the current one has completed), synchronous and wide transfers (asynchronous, narrow), a disconnect in
the middle of a data transfer (the command fails and the bus is reset: no data pointer is restored), data
out phase mismatches (the command fails), more than 33 scatter/gather elements per request, CDBs longer than
16 bytes, more than one logical unit per target, the 40-bit and 64-bit addressing of the 895A (the chip is used with 32-bit
addresses: a request whose buffer has a page above 4 GB goes through one buffer of 128 KB below 4 GB that the driver
allocates, and the data is copied; the adapter says it handles 64-bit addresses, so that the HAL does not double-buffer
for it), the 53C700 family and the wide and ultra chips (53C875, 53C1010: not
emulated, not registered). The interrupt status, the script interrupt code, the data pointer, the residual
count, the reselecting target and the identify and status bytes that the chip and the bus deliver are checked
before they are used.
